# 9 Sep 2026. Forward from 1cbffaa07230, across six deploys in one day
# (#130, #131, #133, #135, #136, #137). PURELY ADDITIVE to everything this app
# reads; the only removals are two `/experiences` query parameters the API had
# never implemented.
#
# Consumed in this change:
#   - `/search` honours a filter with an EMPTY `q` (yuvoy-api#132 → #133). It
#     used to return before reading `destinationKey` or `bookableOn`, so the
#     day chips this app has shipped for a month answered "Nothing on that day"
#     against real departures. Text and filters are independent now; either
#     alone is a real search and together they intersect.
#   - `/search` gains `category` (closed enum, unknown = 400) and
#     `activityType` (open set, unknown = empty page). The asymmetry is
#     deliberate and both are handled: a 400 is a bug in this client, an empty
#     page is a vocabulary that grew since the last deploy.
#   - `Scan.destinationKey` (yuvoy-app#27) — a printed card says where it is.
#     Absent for a market-wide card AND for an unknown code, which is one shape
#     for "no destination" rather than two.
#   - `Attribution.source` gains `web` (yuvoy-api#134 → #135) — our own
#     marketing site, which is neither `referral` (somebody else's) nor
#     `direct` (typed the address).
#   - `POST /operator-applications` gains `privacyAccepted`, `marketingOptIn`
#     and `utm` (yuvoy-web#157). Consumed by yuvoy-web, not by this app.
#
# NOT consumed, and deliberately:
#   - `/experiences` now declares `destinationKey` and `bookableOn`. This app
#     calls that endpoint nowhere — the feed reads `/reels` and search reads
#     `/search` — so the declaration changes nothing here. Its `mode` and `q`
#     were REMOVED; `git grep` confirms neither was ever sent from this repo.
#
# `git ls-remote` was the check for this ref, not `gh api .../commits/master`,
# which served a five-minute-stale SHA twice on 8 Sep and cost a retracted
# issue. And every field above is consumed as OPTIONAL regardless of what
# `required:` says — a pinned contract states what an API WILL send, never what
